<!-- ============ 0 EXECUTIVE VIEW ============ -->
<section id="exec">
  <div class="h2row"><span class="secnum">0</span><h2>Executive View — Read This First</h2></div>
  <div class="callout" style="font-size:1rem;"><b>The agent’s job is to make the honest, rigorous path the laziest path.</b> The incentive plan, supervisor accountability, and the operating-model decision are prerequisites — not afterthoughts. Everything after this page is a menu; strategy is what we refuse to build first.</div>

  <h3>0.1 Five hard truths this plan must respect</h3>
  <div class="tblwrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th style="width:26%">Truth</th><th>Consequence for the plan</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Focus beats completeness</b></td><td>The first 90 days build exactly four things (0.2). Leaderboards, coaching engine, ML confidence scoring and dead-stock revival are deliberately deferred — not because they are wrong, but because a pilot that ships everything proves nothing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>The operating model may be the real problem</b></td><td>SM cradle-to-grave ownership is what makes fake leads and diversion possible. Evaluate the hunter/farmer split (<a href="#model">§2.1</a>) before investing heavily in making the current model more rigorous — we may be building a better whip for a horse that should be replaced.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Incentive math is the load-bearing wall</b></td><td>If a fully-worked conditional-approval case pays the SM less than sourcing a fresh login, no nudge architecture survives contact. Phase 0 audits the actual payout curve; the agent amplifies incentives, it cannot replace them.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Supervisors decide whether this lives</b></td><td>Exception packets fix the supervisor’s <em>effort</em> problem, not their <em>motivation</em> problem. Tie supervisor variable pay to team funnel conversion. One supervisor visibly acting on one packet in week 2 builds more authority than a thousand nudges.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Data readiness gates everything</b></td><td>Most LOS setups lack event-level funnel data, and the real negotiation happens on SMs’ personal phones, invisible to any system. Phase 0 is instrumentation — and official channels must be the laziest path or the data layer becomes theater.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h3>0.2 First 90 days — build only these four</h3>
  <div class="tblwrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th style="width:6%">#</th><th style="width:38%">Build</th><th>Why first</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>1</b></td><td>Funnel event instrumentation + leakage P&amp;L baseline</td><td>Without event-level data the agent is blind and success is unmeasurable; the ₹ baseline is also the business case</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>2</b></td><td>24-hr verification calls on every lead (<a href="#fake">§4</a>)</td><td>Cheapest intervention, immediate fake-lead deterrence, and it starts breaking the SM information monopoly</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>3</b></td><td>Daily top-3 ranked queue (I-3)</td><td>The adoption hook — SMs experience the agent as useful before they experience it as accountable</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>4</b></td><td>Authored commitments + supervisor exception packets (I-6, I-7)</td><td>The rigor core: no silent drops, and supervision becomes cheap enough to actually happen</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="callout blue"><b>Run in parallel (decisions, not builds):</b> incentive payout-curve audit · supervisor variable-pay alignment · matched control branches with pre-registered metrics · pilot in a mid-performing branch with a respected manager — never the best or the worst.</div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 1 PROBLEM ============ -->
<section id="problem">
  <div class="h2row"><span class="secnum">1</span><h2>Problem Snapshot — What Leaks and Why</h2></div>
  <div class="tblwrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Observed symptom</th><th>Root cause</th><th>Psychological driver</th><th>Countered by</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Cases stall silently after login; leak across dept handoffs</td><td>No single owner of the file — each dept owns only its stage</td><td>Diffusion of responsibility (“it’s with credit/legal”)</td><td><a href="#stack"><span class="tag">I-2 forced next action</span></a> <a href="#sla"><span class="tag">I-9 SLA clocks</span></a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>SMs abandon effortful files (legal query, valuation dispute, missing banking)</td><td>Fresh lead = quick reward; stuck file = slow, uncertain reward</td><td>Effort discounting; learned helplessness (“credit will reject anyway”)</td><td><a href="#stack"><span class="tag">I-1 agent does drudgery</span></a> <a href="#stack"><span class="tag">I-3 ranked queue</span></a> <a href="#stack"><span class="tag">I-4 progress framing</span></a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Supervisors miss regular follow-ups</td><td>8–12 SMs × 100+ files; follow-up depends on memory</td><td>Attention scarcity</td><td><a href="#ladder"><span class="tag">I-7 exception-only escalation</span></a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fake leads: CIBIL pulled on “knowns” to show lead numbers</td><td>Lead-count targets reward volume, not quality</td><td>Target gaming (Goodhart’s law)</td><td><a href="#fake"><span class="tag or">§4 verification + quality targets</span></a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stalled cases quietly referred to a competitor for a payout</td><td>SM is sole bridge to customer; deal seen as dead; near-zero detection risk</td><td>Learned helplessness + low perceived detection probability</td><td><a href="#diversion"><span class="tag or">§5 institutional contact + look-back</span></a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>SM productivity &lt; 1 disbursed file / month</td><td>All of the above compounding</td><td>—</td><td><span class="tag gr">Full stack; &gt; 1.5 by Phase 3</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 OPERATING MODEL ============ -->
<section id="model">
  <div class="h2row"><span class="secnum">2</span><h2>Operating Model — One Agent, Three Faces</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">Both integrity problems exist because the SM holds an <em>information monopoly</em> over the customer. The agent breaks that monopoly while doing enough useful work that the salesforce adopts it willingly.</p>
  <div class="om">
    <div class="center"><h4>AGENTIC CASE SHEPHERD</h4><p>One agent instance per loan file · owns the file end-to-end across departments</p></div>
    <div class="face f1">
      <div class="hd">FACE 1 · ASSISTANT <span class="why">earns adoption</span></div>
      <ul><li>Chases valuer / lawyer / customer directly</li><li>Drafts messages &amp; credit-query replies</li><li>Pre-computes counter-offer terms</li><li>AA banking pull + auto-summary for credit</li></ul>
    </div>
    <div class="face f2">
      <div class="hd">FACE 2 · CUSTOMER CHANNEL <span class="why">breaks the info monopoly</span></div>
      <ul><li>24-hr verification call on every lead</li><li>Institutional contact at conditional approval (closes the diversion window)</li><li>Proactive status updates to borrower</li></ul>
    </div>
    <div class="face f3">
      <div class="hd">FACE 3 · PATTERN READER <span class="why">targets consequences</span></div>
      <ul><li>Exception-only supervisor escalation with context packets</li><li>Per-SM behavior signatures · individual vs situational diagnosis</li><li>Auto-prepared weekly 1:1 review packs</li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>2.1 The operating-model decision — evaluate before scaling the rigor stack</h3>
  <p class="lead">The deeper question this plan must answer: <em>should the SM own the file through disbursal at all?</em> The hunter/farmer split — SM sources and hands off at login to a dedicated fulfillment officer or pod — structurally removes both integrity problems. The agent makes this org design affordable for the first time: one fulfillment officer + agent can carry 3–4× the file load.</p>
  <div class="tblwrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th style="width:16%">Dimension</th><th>Current: SM cradle-to-grave</th><th>Alternative: hunter/farmer split</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Ownership</b></td><td>SM owns lead → disbursal; grind competes with fresh-lead reward</td><td>SM sources; fulfillment officer owns login → disbursal — the grind is their only job</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Fake leads</b></td><td>SM gains from showing lead numbers</td><td>Sourcing paid on verified logins that fulfillment accepts — faking becomes pointless</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Diversion</b></td><td>SM is sole customer bridge at the negotiation stage</td><td>Fulfillment owns the customer post-login — the diversion window is <b>structurally closed, not policed</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Supervision</b></td><td>Supervisor chases 8–12 SMs × mixed roles</td><td>Fulfillment pod is a small, measurable unit with one metric: conversion velocity</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Cost</b></td><td>Status quo</td><td>Previously unaffordable; agent-augmented fulfillment changes the math</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="callout green"><b>Recommendation:</b> run one fulfillment-pod pilot in one branch during P1, alongside the rigor stack in the other branch — let the data pick the operating model before region-wide rollout.</div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 3 STACK ============ -->
<section id="stack">
  <div class="h2row"><span class="secnum">3</span><h2>Core Intervention Stack</h2></div>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="icard"><div class="top"><span class="idb">I-1</span><h4>Do-the-work assistant</h4></div>
      <p class="does">Chases valuer / lawyer / customer (WhatsApp + calls); drafts customer messages and credit-query replies; pulls banking via Account Aggregator and auto-summarises; one-tap actions from WhatsApp.</p>
      <div class="meta"><span class="pill who">SM · Credit</span><span class="pill psy">Effort is the enemy — make right action easiest</span><span class="pill met">Stage TAT · actions per nudge</span></div></div>
    <div class="icard"><div class="top"><span class="idb">I-2</span><h4>Forced next action</h4></div>
      <p class="does">No file exists without next action + owner + date. If actionless &gt; 24 hrs, the agent assigns one and notifies the owner.</p>
      <div class="meta"><span class="pill who">All depts</span><span class="pill psy">Kills diffusion of responsibility</span><span class="pill met">% files with live next action → 100%</span></div></div>
    <div class="icard"><div class="top"><span class="idb">I-3</span><h4>Daily top-3 queue</h4></div>
      <p class="does">Morning digest of 3 actions ranked by conversion likelihood × ticket size ÷ effort remaining — makes stuck-but-winnable files attractive.</p>
      <div class="meta"><span class="pill who">SM · Credit</span><span class="pill psy">Counters choice overload &amp; fresh-lead bias</span><span class="pill met">Nudge-to-action rate</span></div></div>
    <div class="icard"><div class="top"><span class="idb">I-4</span><h4>Progress framing</h4></div>
      <p class="does">“This ₹22L file is 80% complete — only legal pending.” Never “12 days old.”</p>
      <div class="meta"><span class="pill who">SM</span><span class="pill psy">Goal-gradient / Zeigarnik effect</span><span class="pill met">Revival rate of &gt;70%-complete files</span></div></div>
    <div class="icard"><div class="top"><span class="idb">I-5</span><h4>Personal loss framing</h4></div>
      <p class="does">“Incentive at risk this month from untouched files: ₹14,600. File #482 dies in 48 hrs.”</p>
      <div class="meta"><span class="pill who">SM</span><span class="pill psy">Loss aversion — specific, personal, time-bound</span><span class="pill met">Response rate to at-risk alerts</span></div></div>
    <div class="icard"><div class="top"><span class="idb">I-6</span><h4>Authored commitments</h4></div>
      <p class="does">A stall requires a stated reason + self-chosen recommit date; the agent holds the SM to their own date. Snooze exists but is finite and visible.</p>
      <div class="meta"><span class="pill who">SM · Credit</span><span class="pill psy">Commitment &amp; consistency; no silent drops</span><span class="pill met">Commitment-kept rate</span></div></div>
    <div class="icard"><div class="top"><span class="idb">I-7</span><h4>Exception-only supervisor escalation</h4></div>
      <p class="does">Supervisor sees only broken commitments and SLA breaches, each with a 2-minute context packet + suggested action; weekly 1:1 agenda auto-prepared per SM.</p>
      <div class="meta"><span class="pill who">Supervisor</span><span class="pill psy">Respects attention scarcity; removes prep cost</span><span class="pill met">Escalations closed &lt; 48 hrs</span></div></div>
    <div class="icard"><div class="top"><span class="idb">I-8</span><h4>Social recognition</h4></div>
      <p class="does">Team stall-recovery leaderboard; “saves” flagged to leadership; clean-pipeline streaks. Negative comparisons stay private.</p>
      <div class="meta"><span class="pill who">SM teams</span><span class="pill psy">Status &gt; small incentives; no public shaming</span><span class="pill met">Stall-recovery rate</span></div></div>
    <div class="icard"><div class="top"><span class="idb">I-9</span><h4>Cross-dept SLA clocks</h4></div>
      <p class="does">Visible per-stage clock (sales / credit / technical / legal / ops); the same nudge–commit–escalate machinery applies to every department.</p>
      <div class="meta"><span class="pill who">All depts</span><span class="pill psy">Precise accountability; perceived fairness</span><span class="pill met">Stage-wise SLA breach %</span></div></div>
    <div class="icard"><div class="top"><span class="idb">I-10</span><h4>Customer-facing agent</h4></div>
      <p class="does">Proactive status updates and next-step requests to the borrower — an informed customer creates internal pull no dashboard can.</p>
      <div class="meta"><span class="pill who">Customer</span><span class="pill psy">External pressure</span><span class="pill met">Customer response rate · NPS</span></div></div>
  </div>

  <h3 id="ladder">3.1 Escalation Ladder — the rigor loop on every pending file</h3>
  <div class="ladder">
    <div class="step s1">
      <div class="box"><b>Agent nudge</b><p>Top-3 queue; file framed as “% complete”; personal incentive at risk.</p></div>
      <div class="trig">No action logged on pending file</div><div class="day">DAY 0</div></div>
    <div class="step s2">
      <div class="box"><b>Authored commitment</b><p>SM states reason + own next-action date — logged, visible.</p></div>
      <div class="trig">Nudge ignored</div><div class="day">DAY 2</div></div>
    <div class="step s3">
      <div class="box"><b>Supervisor packet</b><p>2-minute context: history, reason given, suggested action. Exception-only.</p></div>
      <div class="trig">Commitment broken</div><div class="day">DAY 4</div></div>
    <div class="step s4">
      <div class="box"><b>Second human</b><p>Case reassigned / retention desk takes over customer contact.</p></div>
      <div class="trig">Still stalled</div><div class="day">DAY 7</div></div>
    <div class="step s5">
      <div class="box"><b>Integrity review</b><p>Anomaly evidence packet to integrity function. Statistics flag — humans conclude.</p></div>
      <div class="trig">Pattern detected across files</div><div class="day">ONGOING</div></div>
  </div>

  <h3 id="sla">3.2 Stage SLA Clock <span style="font-weight:400;color:var(--grey);font-size:.85rem;">(indicative — calibrate to own TAT data)</span></h3>
  <div class="tblwrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Owner</th><th>SLA</th><th>Agent action at 80% of SLA</th><th>Agent action on breach</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Lead verification call</td><td><span class="tag gr">Agent (auto)</span></td><td>24 hrs</td><td>—</td><td>Lead flagged unverified; excluded from target credit</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Login + document collection</td><td><span class="tag">SM</span></td><td>3 days</td><td>Doc checklist reminder to SM + customer</td><td><a href="#ladder">Escalation ladder Day 0 starts</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Banking analysis</td><td><span class="tag">Credit</span></td><td>2 days</td><td>Auto-summary already delivered; nudge analyst</td><td>Credit supervisor packet</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Personal discussion (PD)</td><td><span class="tag">Credit</span></td><td>3 days</td><td>Propose 2 PD slots to customer + analyst</td><td>Credit supervisor packet</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Technical valuation</td><td><span class="tag or">Vendor/Tech</span></td><td>4 days</td><td>Agent chases valuer directly</td><td>Vendor SLA report; alternate valuer suggested</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Legal opinion</td><td><span class="tag or">Vendor/Legal</span></td><td>5 days</td><td>Agent chases lawyer; missing-doc list to SM</td><td>Vendor SLA report; regional legal head alert</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Credit query resolution</td><td><span class="tag">SM ↔ Credit</span></td><td>2 days/query</td><td>Draft reply pre-filled from file for SM</td><td>Both-side escalation (query aging is credit’s stall too)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Conditional-approval negotiation</td><td><span class="tag">SM + Retention</span></td><td>5 days</td><td>Counter-offer terms pre-computed; institutional call to customer</td><td><a href="#diversion">Retention desk takes over (§5)</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ops disbursement</td><td><span class="tag">Ops</span></td><td>3 days</td><td>Pending-doc list auto-circulated</td><td>Ops supervisor packet</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 4 FAKE LEADS ============ -->
<section id="fake">
  <div class="h2row"><span class="secnum">4</span><h2>Playbook A — Fake Leads (CIBIL pulls on knowns)</h2></div>
  <div class="callout"><b>Principle: deterrence beats detection.</b> Once every lead is known to get an independent 24-hour verification contact, the expected cost of faking exceeds the benefit.</div>
  <div class="tblwrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Data source</th><th>Agent action</th><th>Escalation threshold</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Customer denies loan interest on 24-hr verification call</td><td>Agent call / WhatsApp outcome</td><td>Lead voided from target credit; logged to SM signature</td><td><span class="tag rd">2 in a quarter → supervisor review</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>CIBIL pulled, zero document movement for 72 hrs</td><td>LOS timeline + bureau log</td><td>Verification re-attempt; file auto-parked as “unverified”</td><td><span class="tag rd">3 in a month → signature flag</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Referrer phone / address clusters across multiple leads</td><td>Lead metadata link analysis</td><td>Cluster report attached to each member lead</td><td><span class="tag rd">Any cluster ≥ 3 leads → evidence packet</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lead spike in final 3 days of target period</td><td>LOS lead-creation timestamps</td><td>Spike leads get priority verification</td><td><span class="tag rd">2 consecutive months → 1:1 item</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>CIBIL-pull-to-login conversion far below branch norm</td><td>Bureau logs vs LOS</td><td>Included in monthly SM behavior signature</td><td><span class="tag rd">Statistical outlier vs branch/vintage peers</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <h3>4.1 Root-cause fixes — without these, the agent just teaches better faking</h3>
  <div class="tblwrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th style="width:30%">Fix</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Retire lead-count targets</b></td><td>Shift SM scorecard weight to quality metrics: verified-engagement rate, login-to-sanction rate, disbursal count</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Announce the verification policy</b></td><td>“Every lead is contacted within 24 hours” communicated openly — the deterrent is the certainty, not the punishment</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Cost visibility</b></td><td>Monthly report of bureau-pull cost wasted on never-progressed leads, by branch</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 DIVERSION ============ -->
<section id="diversion">
  <div class="h2row"><span class="secnum">5</span><h2>Playbook B — Lead Diversion to Competitors</h2></div>
  <div class="callout"><b>Diversion needs three conditions:</b> the SM believes the deal is dead here · negotiation effort is high · detection risk is near zero. Attack all three. Highest-risk window: <b>immediately after credit imposes conditions.</b></div>
  <div class="tblwrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Lever</th><th>Trigger / window</th><th>Agent action</th><th>Detection &amp; consequence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Close the window</b></td><td>Credit issues conditional approval</td><td>Customer hears conditions from the institution within hours — agent call/WhatsApp explaining conditions + the path to approval. SM no longer the sole bridge</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Lower the effort</b></td><td>Negotiation-stage file idle 48 hrs</td><td>Agent pre-computes restructure options that satisfy credit (lower LTV, co-applicant, tenor) and hands SM a ready script</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Make honesty pay more</b></td><td>Conditional case revived to disbursal</td><td>Revival bonus weighted above fresh-file sourcing in SM incentives</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Second human</b></td><td>Stall persists 48 hrs post-conditions</td><td>Auto-route to supervisor / central retention desk — a second relationship with the customer</td><td>One person can no longer quietly kill a file</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Look-back with teeth</b></td><td>Case withdrawn / expired post-approval</td><td>Exit call to customer (“what made you drop?”); bureau re-pull at 45–60 days</td><td><span class="tag rd">New competitor mortgage tradeline → logged to SM signature</span>; policy announced, so certainty deters</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Pattern consequence</b></td><td>≥ 2 look-back hits for one SM</td><td>Evidence packet compiled</td><td><span class="tag rd">Integrity function investigates; agent never accuses</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 DIAGNOSIS ============ -->
<section id="diagnosis">
  <div class="h2row"><span class="secnum">6</span><h2>Individual vs Situational — Diagnose Before Acting</h2></div>
  <div class="callout blue">Baseline every signal against <b>peers, branch, and vintage</b> — never against absolutes. A branch-wide pattern is a process problem; a single-SM deviation is a conduct question.</div>
  <div class="tblwrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Pattern observed</th><th>Likely diagnosis</th><th>System response</th><th>Human owner</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Entire branch stalls at same stage (e.g., legal)</td><td><span class="tag gr">Situational</span> vendor or local process issue</td><td>Root-cause report; vendor SLA action; do <b>not</b> police individuals</td><td>Ops / Regional head</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One SM deviates sharply from branch norm on fake-lead or diversion signals</td><td><span class="tag rd">Individual</span></td><td>Quietly raise verification tier; compile evidence packet</td><td>Supervisor → Integrity</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stall reasons cluster on one credit condition across regions</td><td><span class="tag">Product / policy</span></td><td>Credit-policy feedback report from authored stall reasons</td><td>Credit policy head</td></tr>
      <tr><td>New-joiner SM with low activity, no integrity signals</td><td><span class="tag gr">Capability, not intent</span></td><td>Coaching nudges; buddy assignment; lighter framing</td><td>Supervisor</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Seasonal / geographic dip across teams</td><td><span class="tag gr">Situational</span></td><td>Recalibrate targets and SLAs for the period</td><td>Business head</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <h3>6.1 Risk-tiered treatment — underwriting applied inward</h3>
  <div class="tblwrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Trust tier</th><th>Criteria</th><th>Nudge intensity</th><th>Verification intensity</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="tag gr">Trusted</span></td><td>Clean 6-month signature; high commitment-kept rate</td><td>Light touch; more autonomy; recognition-heavy</td><td>Standard 24-hr call only</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag">Standard</span></td><td>Default for all</td><td>Full ladder (<a href="#ladder">§3.1</a>)</td><td>Standard + random look-backs</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag rd">Elevated</span></td><td>Signature flags pending review</td><td>Full ladder, faster escalation</td><td>Enhanced: agent-direct customer contact on all files; look-back on every lost case — experienced as “more support”, not accusation</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 7 GUARDRAILS ============ -->
<section id="guardrails">
  <div class="h2row"><span class="secnum">7</span><h2>Guardrails — How This System Fails If Misdesigned</h2></div>
  <div class="guards">
    <div class="guard"><b>Alert fatigue → agent gets muted</b><p>Max 3 nudges / person / day; escalate through channel and tone, never volume.</p></div>
    <div class="guard"><b>Seen as surveillance → subverted</b><p>Assistant features (I-1) ship first and visibly save time; keep ≥ 3:1 ratio of recognition to corrective messages.</p></div>
    <div class="guard"><b>Goodhart on activity</b><p>Never reward “actions logged”; reward outcomes only — conversion, TAT, stall recovery.</p></div>
    <div class="guard"><b>False accusation destroys trust</b><p>Agent flags with evidence; humans conclude. No automated integrity labels, ever.</p></div>
    <div class="guard"><b>Legitimate waits criminalised</b><p>Finite, visible snooze with authored reason (customer travelling, title dispute) — a sanctioned path prevents invented ones.</p></div>
    <div class="guard"><b>Rigor only on sales → seen as unfair</b><p>Credit, technical, legal and ops run on the same clocks and ladders (I-9).</p></div>
    <div class="guard"><b>Rigor degrades underwriting</b><p>Track early delinquency of revived / negotiated files vs normal originations — the balancing metric that keeps the whole program honest.</p></div>
    <div class="guard"><b>Fails socially before technically</b><p>Name the agent; ship WhatsApp-first; recruit 2–3 respected SMs as co-design champions in pilot branches; track tool-eNPS.</p></div>
    <div class="guard"><b>Consequences never land → nudges become wallpaper</b><p>Authority is built by visible action, not messages: a supervisor must act on an exception packet in week 2. Track packet-to-action rate as a leading indicator.</p></div>
    <div class="guard"><b>Evidence packet used for public punishment</b><p>Integrity outcomes must be quiet, procedural and visibly fair — one humiliating public firing and every SM starves the system of data permanently.</p></div>
    <div class="guard"><b>Shadow process on personal phones</b><p>The real negotiation happens off-system today. Official channels must be the laziest path (one-tap, vernacular, pre-drafted); track in-channel contact share.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

